<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ONE KEY, ON PURPOSE.

  This file exists to be selected in ESign's "Custom signature entitlements" box, on a phone with
  no computer to write a plist on. It is not the app's real entitlements file; that is
  Continuum.entitlements next to this one.

  Only `get-task-allow` is here because it is the only key that decides whether a recompiler is
  possible on iOS, and because every ADDITIONAL key is another reason for installd to reject the
  signed app. A minimal file has the best chance of installing at all, and installing is the whole
  question being tested.

  Three outcomes, all of them answers:

    will not install                 the provisioning profile refuses get-task-allow. A wall, not
                                     a mistake, and the end of the road for a recompiler on that
                                     signing identity.
    installs, JIT line says MISSING  the signer did not apply this file.
    installs, JIT line says present  it worked.

  The app reads its own signature and prints which one happened; see jit_probe.rs.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
